  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nAs of 18z, there are currently some isolated showers lingering over \nportions of south central and east central SD. These showers are \nslowly moving around and then dissipating after not too long. The \nenvironment has a touch of instability, with between 250-500 J/kg \nover south central and east central SD. This could help with the \ndevelopment of occasional showers and weak & non-severe storms over \nportions of central and east central SD this afternoon and evening. \nOtherwise, central and northeastern SD will stay dry as showers and \nstorms stay to the south and west through the overnight hours. \n\nThe upper-level ridge that had kept northwest flow over SD through \nthe past week has finally shifted to the east over the southeastern \nUS. With this ridge moving east, the upper-level flow has switched \nto be from the southwest through the weekend. This southwest flow \nwill start to push the wildfire smoke aloft and at the surface out \nof central and northeastern SD today and into Friday. There could be \na bit of smoke aloft over northern SD through the evening before it \nmoves out. Temperatures today and into the weekend will be about 5-\n10 degrees below normal, with highs in the 70s. \n\nThe lee of the Rockies surface trough will slowly be pushed to the \neast by the southwest flow Friday. This surface boundary is forecast \nto move over central SD late Friday afternoon and into the evening. \nThis will provide support for the next chance for showers and storms \nto develop, as the boundary will move into an environment with lots \nof moisture and some weak instability. The NAM and RAP show CAPE \nvalues in western SD getting up into the 1000s J/kg while central SD \nwill see values around 500 J/kg. While the values are a bit weaker, \nit could still be enough to help storms develop in central SD or \nhave storms develop in western SD and then move into central SD. \nThere does look to be lower lapse rates and shear values, which \nhelps to increase the confidence that if any storms develop, then \nthey will stay non-severe. These storms could have some stronger \nwind gusts at times. As the surface boundary shifts east over \nnortheastern SD during the overnight hours, the storms look to \ndissipate. This is because the environment is even weaker and there \nis nothing to support the storm, such as a low level jet which will \ndevelop in ND. A cold front along the back side of this surface \nboundary looks to move through central and northeastern SD Saturday \nmorning. This could cause an isolated shower or two to pop up \nthrough the day Saturday.",
